Postgres:
-- 4 tables, joined for every order detail query
orders(id, user_id, status, total, created_at)
order_items(id, order_id, product_id, quantity, unit_price)
users(id, name, email)
products(id, name, sku, price)
MongoDB:
// one collection, denormalised
orders {
_id, user_id,
user_email_at_order: "...", // extended reference
status, total, created_at,
items: [
{ p: product_id, name_at_order: "...", price_at_order: ..., qty: ... }
]
}
users { _id, name, email }
products { _id, name, sku, price }
The trade-off: the join you ran 1,000× per second goes away; product name changes don't retroactively rewrite past orders (which is usually correct anyway). A handful of cross-order reports use $lookup now and run nightly via $merge.

